Exercise 4.22 asks whether a structured set \(\mathbf{N}^*\) satisfying only Definition 4.5(i) and (ii) must contain a subset satisfying (i), (ii), and (iii), with the hint to take a least fixed point in \(P\mathbf{N}^*\), and how the axioms of set theory provide some such \(\mathbf{N}^*\). `genOp` sends a subset to the singleton of zero together with the successor image. `nats` is the least fixed point of that operator. `zero_mem_nats` and `succ_mem_nats` place zero and successors in `nats` by testing an arbitrary pre-fixed set against the corresponding clause of `genOp`. `genOp_monotone` is the inclusion argument for those two clauses, and `genOp_nats` is the resulting fixed-point equation. `nats_induction` says that any set containing zero and closed under successor contains `nats`, because that set is pre-fixed and the least fixed point lies under every pre-fixed point. `Nat'` is the subtype of elements of `nats`, `subZero` and `subSucc` are the inherited zero and successor, and `peanoSub` is a Peano model on that subtype: the ambient laws that zero is never a successor and that successor is injective pass to underlying elements, and induction pulls a subset of the subtype back to the ambient type, checks that the pullback contains zero and is closed under successor, and applies `nats_induction`. `exists_peano_submodel` is the existence of that model, and `example_422` is that statement. `natPeano` separately checks that the standard naturals satisfy all three Peano clauses, by distinctness of zero from successors, injectivity of successor, and induction. The glosses present those naturals as a witness that some partial model exists and do not derive the witness from the axiom of infinity. **Theorem:** partial — the carved-out subtype is a Peano model whenever the ambient zero is never a successor and successor is injective; existence of an ambient model is the standard naturals, not a derivation from the axiom of infinity. **Proof:** partial — pre-fixedness of an inductive set and inheritance of (i) and (ii) are the least-fixed-point argument the hint asks for, while the existence half checks the three clauses for \(\mathbf{N}\) and does not mention an infinity axiom.
